.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH ckman-oath-challenge-response 1  ckman 
.SH NAME
ckman\-oath\-challenge\-response \- Compute an HMAC\-SHA1 challenge\-response from a PASS slot
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBckman\-oath\-challenge\-response\fR [\fB\-t\fR|\fB\-\-text\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISLOT\fR> <\fICHALLENGE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Compute an HMAC\-SHA1 challenge\-response from a PASS slot.
.PP
The slot must be configured as HMAC\-SHA1 (see `config pass set`).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-text\fR
Treat the challenge as a plain UTF\-8 string instead of hex
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISLOT\fR>
PASS HMAC\-SHA1 slot to answer from
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
short
.IP \(bu 2
long
.RE
.TP
<\fICHALLENGE\fR>
The challenge, hex\-encoded (a plain string with \-\-text)
